{
  "entity1": {
    "feedback": "Revise before racing. This candidate is the same design as iterations 6 and 7, which never raced, so my earlier scale-matching request may not have reached the Optimizer. The geometry is a sound single-variable test. Seven sensors at x = -0.075 to +0.075 m in 0.025 m steps (y 0.06, sensor_size 0.012) keep at least one 12 mm footprint on the 25 mm tape across the span. Iteration 3 (best raced, 361.4) finished the oval 20 of 20 at 1.86 s, while slalom showed about one line-loss event per run with progress stuck near 0.10, which points to the tape leaving the five-sensor span (iterations 3 and 5). The problem is the weights. With outer weights of ±1.5, the signal can reach 1.5 where iteration 3 reached 1.0, so the effective proportional gain rises about 1.5x. Iteration 2 showed that a higher effective gain with a larger footprint cut oval finishes from 0.9 to 0.55 and raised steering_oscillation to 0.27. This candidate could repeat that damage and would confound the span test. Keep the signal scale equal to iteration 3 by spreading the weights across ±1.0, and keep the controller at iteration 3 values (base_speed 1.1, kp 2.5, ki 0.02, kd 0.8, last_direction). Judge the next race per track. If slalom and s-bend still leave the line with line_loss_events near 1 and max_error at or above 0.06 m, coverage is not the limit and the next step is turn authority, kp about 3.0 on the same scale-matched weights, not the ±1.5 weights. If oval steering_oscillation rises above 0.3 with scale-matched weights, lower kp toward 2.0 before changing the array.",
    "requested_changes": [
      "Set controller.sensor_weights to a scale-matched spread across ±1.0, for example [-1.0, -0.67, -0.33, 0.0, 0.33, 0.67, 1.0], so the signal peak matches iteration 3. Iteration 2 showed that a higher effective gain cut oval finishes from 0.9 to 0.55.",
      "Keep the seven sensors at x = -0.075, -0.05, -0.025, 0, 0.025, 0.05, 0.075 m, y 0.06, sensor_size 0.012, and keep kp 2.5, kd 0.8, ki 0.02, base_speed 1.1, line_loss last_direction. Iteration 3 (same gains, five sensors at ±0.06) finished 20 of 20 on the oval, so this isolates the span effect.",
      "If the scale-matched race still ends slalom and s-bend off track with line_loss_events near 1 and max_error at or above 0.06 m, raise kp from 2.5 to about 3.0 on the same scale-matched weights, not on the ±1.5 weights."
    ],
    "decision": "revise"
  },
  "_presentation": {
    "entity1": {
      "label": "Evaluator: scale weights",
      "notes": "Revise: the seven-sensor span is a sound test, but outer weights of ±1.5 raise effective gain about 1.5x, which iteration 2 showed can cut oval finishes. Requests weights scaled to ±1.0 with all other iteration 3 settings held, so the span is the only change; based on iterations 2, 3 and 5."
    }
  }
}
